**Whole task:** grip a 3.5 mm patch cable, insert it into a target jack, verify it is patched, and pull it back out.

**Our design.** The PAROL6 manual stops at pick-and-place, so this milestone is ours.

## Parts

- A cable-holding gripper: TPU 75A fingers, or a printed plug holder on a compliant mount ([Custom tools](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/end-effectors/custom-tools/)).
- Patch cables.
- A cable rack or nest to pick from.

<TimelapseLinks milestone="m6-patch-a-cable" />

## Concepts

- **Peg-in-hole insertion:** chamfers, lateral compliance, and why open-loop steppers cannot feel force. An overloaded stepper loses steps silently ([Drives, ratios and resolution](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/03-technical-data/drives-and-ratios/)).
- **Spiral-search and wiggle strategies.**
- **Detection without a force sensor.** Verify the patch electrically: send an LFO through the new cable and read it at the destination. Our design.

## How-to

- Our insertion routine, written as a completion task on the [Python API](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/programming/02-python-api/).
- The measured jack positions from [M5](/lore/m5-touch-the-skiff).

## Practice

- Insertion trials across jack positions.
- Failure-mode drills: a missed jack, a cable caught on a knob.

## Done check

Nine of 10 inserts and 10 of 10 removals across three different jacks, each verified electrically. No damage to the jacks.

## Terms

- **peg-in-hole**: the robotics name for inserting one part into a close-fitting hole, here a 3.5 mm plug into a jack. Our design; the PAROL6 docs do not cover it.
- **spiral search**: moving the plug in a widening spiral around the expected jack position until it drops in. Our design.

